prompt:
A real photograph of a small square of Lisbon on a cool November evening around Saint Martin's day, in a tall vertical picture made of one single continuous view, caught as it happens, as a passer-by would take it with a good phone camera. Taken with a 24 mm lens at standing eye height from the edge of the square, looking at the chestnut cart and the tram stop behind it, everything in sharp focus. The orange glow of street lamps and the blue evening sky; the textures of real black iron, coal, smoke, roasted chestnut shells, newspaper, wool, cotton, denim and skin.

The first reference picture shows the young man from the front, the second shows him from his side: his face, his wavy dark-brown hair, his short stubble, his build, his height and his clothes, the royal-blue hooded sweatshirt with its red band across the chest, the white t-shirt, the cream scarf with small blue embroidered flowers, the dark-blue jeans and the white canvas sneakers, stay exactly as in them.

The third reference picture shows the waitress, the young woman with round red glasses: her face, her dark hair pinned up, her round red glasses and her build stay exactly as in it.

Every surface in the picture is plain: the paper cones are plain grey paper, the cart is plain black iron, the bags are plain single colours, and the clothes are plain cloth. Each object stands clearly on its own, with a band of bare pavement or bare wall around it, so that the eye can tell at once where everything is.

The picture is built in three bands, from the bottom edge to the top edge.

THE FOREGROUND, along the bottom of the picture:
- the chestnut seller's black iron cart stands on the right of the middle, shaped like a little steam engine with a chimney, chestnuts roasting on the coals;
- the seller, a man of seventy in a flat cap and a scarf, holds out a paper cone full of hot chestnuts;
- the young man of the reference pictures stands just left of the cart, seen whole from his head to his white sneakers; his body is turned in three-quarter view towards the right of the picture; a great cloud of thick white smoke from the chimney has wrapped him completely, so that only his arm sticking out of the cloud is clearly seen, holding a paper cone of chestnuts.

THE MIDDLE OF THE SQUARE, from left to right:
- on the left, the waitress of the third reference picture, in a red wool coat, laughs and waves the smoke away with her hand;
- a wooden bench with fallen yellow leaves under it;
- a tram stop with a little glass shelter, an old couple waiting in it.

THE BACK OF THE SQUARE, across the upper third of the picture:
- old houses with lit windows and wrought-iron balconies;
- a tall tree with yellow autumn leaves;
- an old black iron street lamp glowing orange, and a deep blue evening sky.

The square is calm and cosy: between the people, the same bare pavement and the same bare walls.
